    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, our most potent engine is **Lawrence Butler**, whose score of 67.01 leads the unit despite a suboptimal #9 spot in the order. He benefits from a high-upside matchup against Giolito (5.01 xERA), and his elite speed profile is perfectly leveraged against a favorable defensive pairing, making him our primary target for SB accumulation. **Bryson Stott** (65.79) follows closely behind, capitalizing on a weak Cecconi delivery and his own elite sprint speed. While the wind-in factor at his park is a slight drag, **Bryson Stott** remains a high-floor start because of the specific tactical advantage he holds over the opposing catcher\u2019s pop time. Similarly, **Masyn Winn** (64.02) is a forced start against the erratic Petty; his ability to exploit the high xERA creates an ideal environment for his offensive floor to reach \"Strong\" territory.\n\nThe decision to start **Daulton Varsho** (50.42) over other depth options was purely a bet on his platoon profile, even with the daunting shadow of Skenes (2.96 xERA) on the mound. **Daulton Varsho** narrowly edged out **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (52.07) in terms of categorical utility; while **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** is an elite talent, the matchup against Wheeler is a significant net-negative that drags his day-to-day efficiency score down by 19% on SP skill alone. We are closely monitoring the lineup status of **Gavin Sheets** and **Tyler Freeman**; both are currently projected for the lineup, but any unexpected scratch will force us to re-evaluate our bench depth, which remains depleted due to the IL statuses of **Cal Raleigh**, **Yainer Diaz**, and **Dylan Beavers**, as well as the unfortunate rainout for **Taylor Ward**. Given the scarcity of SBs, we are closely watching **Drew Gilbert** (43.47); while his score is marginal, his speed tools keep him in the mix as a secondary hedge against our core hitters in a game with favorable wind conditions.",
